Z||000006||Joe4x4||11-27-2000||02:04 PM||||3.4 sounds like very good for $ 50<BR>What car would be best ?<BR>Does it have to be front wheel drive?<BR>and will I have to use the tranny from that car or can it work with fiero tranny?<P>Thanks for helping  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||11-27-2000||03:38 PM||||The 3.4 comes in 93-95 Camaros and Firebirds. It is a pushrod engine, not the overhead cam engine. Overhead cams engine can be done, but is more difficult then the pushrod 3.4. I am putting in a 3.4 this January. It will bolt to the Fiero tranny, but auto and manuals. If you have an auto, you must cut away a little bit of the 3.4 block to clear the tranny, but it works. You can make it look just like the Fiero engine becuase you use everything from the original engine from the heads up. I will have some more information about the setup I am doing and how I am going to be doing it, on my website maybe tomarrow or the next day. Also, you will be very luck to find the 3.4 engine for $50. I am going to be paying $750 for mine with under 40K on it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||11-27-2000||03:59 PM||||There may be several "schools of thought" working here. While a complete motor swap (e.g., SBC, 3800, 3800 SC, etc) sounds great, it can be rather costly. The cheapest I've ever heard for a complete swap was about $1000. That low cost was due to the owner doing all the labor and fabrication himself AND getting a cheap motor.<P>As far as the 2.8 upgrade, I personally like the 3.4. It's practically a bolt in replacement for the 2.8. Still some fabrication skills are required or must be purchased (i.e., starter and A/C mount).<P>The absolute cheapest I can see is to upgrade to a 3.1. If you have a motor in good shape with good compression, you can find a good crankshaft and flexplate or flywheel from a bone yard donor car and, with a new set of rod and main bearings, just replace your old 2.8 crankshaft and flexplate/flywheel. Of course,you still have to pop for a pan gasket and replace the bearings but EVERYTHING ELSE IS BOLT ON. If you can handle all the labor yourself, you can probably do the 3.1 conversion on your good 2.8 for around $200.<P>The next cheapest upgrade would be to replace your old rocker arms with 1.6 ratio rockers. Figure another $150 to $250 if you go with roller rockers. I guestamate that the 3.1 with the 1.6 upgrage to the 2.8 would get you about 30-40 HP. How about it... anyone with a decktop dyno?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000009||Joe4x4||11-27-2000||05:11 PM||||Seanh<P>I like  the 3.4 swap ...<BR>Do you have the motor already ?<BR>can you keep informed as to how you <BR>are going to do the swap.<BR>and the mods you have to do <BR>so than I can follow you steps.<P>Seanh<P>how about this 454 ...out of my Show Truck<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Engine_454_1.JPG"> <P><BR>Hmmmm  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||11-27-2000||05:27 PM||||It's been my experience that used car parts dealers always lie about the actual mileage and condition of the engines they sell. Don't believe them if they say that an engine has 50k. There worse than car sales people of which is another experience of mine. They will say anything to get $$$ and a sale from you!||||reg||1
Z||000011||mshill||11-27-2000||06:00 PM||||88formula,  they may have lied about this engine having 50K on it but when I took the top end off of it everything was just (barely) slightly bronzed.  It was a beautiful sight.<P>Joe4x4, I have my long block.  I have stripped off the intake manifold, pulley, damper and timing cover.  I am waiting for a very nice forum member to ship me the kit needed to drill the starter holes.  After that I just need to get a stud welded to a bracket for the AC bracket to mount to and it will be time to assemble the top end and start prepping the cradle/transmission for the new motor.  It needs to be cleaned up and have poly bushing put on the a-arms.  I purchased a used 88 V6 flywheel for $60 shipped, which I will pay $10 to have resurfaced.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||11-27-2000||07:05 PM||||Sure, I am going to document the whole thing, just so I can remember how much was put into this car when (If ever) I decide to sell it and some one tires to offer me squat for it. I am going to take the car off the road the first week of January and begin the complete rebuilding of the car in our shop. (To explain "our shop" a little better, when we moved to our house it had a 2 car grauge and we had an additional 3 car grauge built on the out side of the existing grauge. I love it, its heated because it is under the 2nd floor of the house, and is great to work on the car in)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I am going to take the car completely apart to remove all the rust and rebuild all the cars systems. I will be done with the car around April/May. I dont have the engine yet, but a lead on one in Denver. I am going to do the block mods myself. I have a brother who owns his own machine shop. I just found out a few weeks ago he did have his own shop. I will be spending a few weeks down there with him in colorado makeing all the brackets for the engine and other things I will need on the car like brakes. I will be changing the cam in the 3.4 to a more aggressive one. I will be useing a custom built and ported set of heads. I will also be getting a set of custom built headers from Mike LeCompte (HYPTNOTiSE on the Forum). Also I will be doing some other small things to the engine like bigger injectors, Crane ingition system, custom PROM chip, and some other things that are not comming to mind right now. I will chart the progress of the rebuild once I get started on my website, but I tomarrow I am going to finish the 3.4 swap page on my site to include all the things I am doing to the engine and the process for installing it. I am looking at about $6500-7000 for the complete rebuild. That includes the engine and everthing else I am doing to car, and some extra money for those things you never think of until you see them.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||11-27-2000||09:45 PM||||Very rarely will you get out all the $$ you put in. All too often you see $8000 invested will take $5000.||||reg||1
Z||000014||HYPTNOTiSE||11-28-2000||04:43 AM||||Hey 88 Formula!!! "There worse than car sales people of which is another experience of mine. They will say anything to get $$$ and a sale from you!"<P>I resemble that remark.  Dont think so badly about every used car salesman out there. We're not all bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Oh yeah while i'm on here i might as well try to squeeze some bucks out of some of you 3.4 guys :P  I have the setup to relocate the starter to the other side of the block, by setting up the plate, drilling then tapping the threads for the starter bolts. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/assembledplateS.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/platesetupS.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MikeDrillingS_1.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tappingholesS.jpg"> ||||reg||1
